gosh seam 24: the network reads the numbers in what it opens a byte at a time, by two proved functions (Network::Imp::be16_at, be64_at).

For:   REQ22/SHAPE-1 (a refactor of the core's code, where warranted: the owner, 2026-11-28, "go that
       way", of proving what reads bytes from anyone, and refactoring where reading the code as
       written would cost more than about a day). After decrypt, two parses read what it gives:
       a packet's two timestamps (Packet( const Message& )) and a fragment's id and number
       (Fragment( const std::string& )). Each read them through a pointer to a wider type,
       `(uint16_t*)text.data()`, which C++ leaves undefined (a std::string's chars read as
       uint16_t: the strict aliasing rule), though every compiler gosh is built with does what is
       meant. That cannot be proved; a read a byte at a time can.
What:  src/network/imp/bytes.h, new: be16_at( text, n, at ) and be64_at( text, n, at ), the number of
       the 2 or 8 bytes at `at` of the n bytes of text, the first the highest, and 0 where they are
       not all there; proved for every view and every place (seam/proofs/). The two constructors
       read by them, where they read through the wider pointers and be16toh, be64toh: the same
       numbers, on any machine. Each still tests its length first as it did (dos_assert,
       fatal_assert), so the 0 is never given there. No byte of the protocol changes.
Where: src/network/imp/bytes.h (new); src/network/imp/network.cc (Packet); src/network/imp/transportfragment.cc
       (Fragment); src/network/Makefile.am (the header listed).
Kind:  a refactor inside the transport (Network::Imp), and a new file there.
From:  the core at the pin: src/network/network.cc (Packet::Packet( const Message& )) and
       src/network/transportfragment.cc (Fragment::Fragment( const std::string& )), both moved under
       imp/ by patch 14.
Pin:   upstream mosh at decd9b705eb81626f694335b8d5940538beb06da; cut against the result of the patch before it.

diff --git a/src/network/imp/bytes.h b/src/network/imp/bytes.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/network/imp/bytes.h
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+/*
+ * The network's numbers read from bytes as they cross (gosh seam 24): big-endian, a byte at a time,
+ * so that no byte of a std::string is read through a pointer to a wider type (which C++ leaves
+ * undefined). Proved (seam/proofs/be16_at.from, be64_at.from): for every view and every place, no read outside it.
+ */
+#ifndef NETWORK_IMP_BYTES_H
+#define NETWORK_IMP_BYTES_H
+
+#include <cstddef>
+#include <cstdint>
+
+namespace Network {
+namespace Imp {
+
+/* The number of the 2 bytes at `at` of the n bytes of text, the first the higher; 0 if they are
+   not both there. */
+inline uint16_t be16_at( const char* text, size_t n, uint64_t at )
+{
+  const unsigned char* b = reinterpret_cast<const unsigned char*>( text );
+  if ( at > n || n - at < 2 ) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+  return static_cast<uint16_t>( ( static_cast<uint32_t>( b[at] ) << 8 ) | static_cast<uint32_t>( b[at + 1] ) );
+}
+
+/* The number of the 8 bytes at `at` of the n bytes of text, the first the highest; 0 if they are
+   not all there. */
+inline uint64_t be64_at( const char* text, size_t n, uint64_t at )
+{
+  const unsigned char* b = reinterpret_cast<const unsigned char*>( text );
+  if ( at > n || n - at < 8 ) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+  return ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at] ) << 56 ) | ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 1] ) << 48 )
+         | ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 2] ) << 40 ) | ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 3] ) << 32 )
+         | ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 4] ) << 24 ) | ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 5] ) << 16 )
+         | ( static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 6] ) << 8 ) | static_cast<uint64_t>( b[at + 7] );
+}
+
+}
+}
+
+#endif
